---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const morphCfg = {
  steps: [
    { math: '1 · Morphemes: the smallest meaning units', note: 'un-believ-abl-y: four morphemes (not + believe + able + adverb-maker). Free morphemes stand alone (believe); bound ones attach (un-, -y). Words are built structures, not atoms.' },
    { math: '2 · Inflection vs derivation', note: 'Inflection changes GRAMMAR without new words (cat/cats, walk/walked — same lexeme). Derivation makes NEW words (teach → teacher, happy → unhappy). English inflection is thin (~6 endings); Latin had hundreds.' },
    { math: '3 · Agglutination and polysynthesis', note: 'Turkish glues morphemes transparently (ev-ler-den-iz: house-PL-ABL-—"from the houses"); Inuktitut packs whole sentences into one word. The typology range is enormous — English is on the analytic end.' },
    { math: '4 · Productivity: living vs dead morphemes', note: '-ness still makes new words (binge-watching-ness?); -th barely does (warmth, depth — frozen). Some affixes are productive, some fossils — morphologists measure which is which.' },
  ],
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/linguistics/intermediate/morphology">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Morphology studies <strong>how words are built from meaning units</strong> — morphemes — and
    how languages range from analytic (one morpheme per word: Chinese) to polysynthetic (whole
    sentences per word: Inuktitut). English sits mid-range: thin inflection, rich derivation,
    and a habit of borrowing morphemes from Latin and Greek.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="morphemes, the inflection/derivation split, the typology range, productivity" widget="equationStep" config={morphCfg} />

  <h2>Why morphology matters beyond description</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Reading and spelling:</strong> English spelling encodes morphemes, not just sounds — "electric" / "electricity" keeps the same spelling despite sound changes because the morpheme is the same. Morphological awareness is a literacy superpower.</li>
    <li><strong>Machine translation's old enemy:</strong> agglutinative languages (Turkish, Finnish) explode the word-count — one Finnish word can need a sentence of English. Morphological analysers were NLP's first hard problem.</li>
    <li><strong>Word formation is creative:</strong> new words form by derivation (googleable), compounding (snowmageddon), blending (brunch), backformation (burgle from burglar). The lexicon is alive, and morphology is its workshop.</li>
    <li><strong>Language typology:</strong> the morpheme-per-word spectrum (analytic → agglutinative → fusional → polysynthetic) is one of linguistics' main classification axes — the how-language-works lesson's map, zoomed in.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Lieber, Introducing Morphology; Berko (1958); Haspelmath & Sims, Understanding Morphology.</p>
</TopicPage>
